// Settings that correspond to the dialog "Configuration Categories" -> "Expert Settings" → "System Hardening"
- name="kernel-option-duurn" \
  dialog-name="System Hardening" \
  short-description="Restrict unprivileged user namespaces to the programs that need them" \
  long-description="Restrict unprivileged user namespaces: User namespaces let an unprivileged process gain capabilities inside a namespace of its own, and they are a historically common ingredient of local privilege escalations — of the sixteen Linux kernel entries CISA added to its Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalogue in three years, six are exploitable through them. Sandboxes need them all the same: every Flatpak, and Bubblejail too, runs bubblewrap to build its outer sandbox, and bubblewrap creates a user namespace. Ditana therefore neither switches them off for everybody nor on for everybody. With this option active, the kernel refuses a user namespace to every program except those a setting has explicitly asked for — today that is bubblewrap, and each request is declared next to the package that needs it. The permission is enforced by ditana-userns-guard, which attaches a BPF program to the kernel's own userns_create hook and is installed together with this option. Should that program ever fail to load, the system keeps unprivileged user namespaces switched off entirely, so a failure costs you your sandboxes and never your protection. Turn this option off only if you want unprivileged user namespaces available to every program on the system." \
  spdx-identifiers="GPL-2.0-only" \
  default-value=#true {
    arch-packages "ditana-userns-guard"
    \ // The sysctl is written as 0 even though the guard needs it at 1, and that
    \ // is the whole safety argument rather than an oversight. A BPF LSM hook can
    \ // only ever deny; it cannot grant. So the allowlist is worthless while the
    \ // kernel refuses every unprivileged user namespace outright, and the guard's
    \ // service unit raises the value itself once the program is verifiably
    \ // attached. A machine that cannot load it therefore boots as locked as it
    \ // was installed.
    sysctl {
        - "kernel.unprivileged_userns_clone" "0"
    }
    chroot-script "systemctl enable ditana-userns-guard.service"
  }

- name="enable-unprivileged-namespaces" \
  \ // The complement of kernel-option-duurn, and the only place where Ditana sets
  \ // this value to 1 from the configuration. The two cannot both be enabled,
  \ // because this one's default is the negation of the other's value — and if a
  \ // future edit ever breaks that, the installer refuses two settings writing the
  \ // same key differently rather than letting the last one win unseen.
  default-value="`NOT kernel-option-duurn`" {
    sysctl {
        - "kernel.unprivileged_userns_clone" "1"
    }
  }

- name="sysctl-kexec-disabled" \
  dialog-name="System Hardening" \
  short-description="Disable kexec (kernel.kexec_load_disabled=1)" \
  long-description="Disable kexec: Prevents loading a replacement kernel at runtime via the kexec system call. An attacker with sufficient privileges could use kexec to boot a malicious kernel, bypassing Secure Boot and other boot-time protections. Once set to 1 at runtime, this cannot be changed back without a reboot." \
  spdx-identifiers="GPL-2.0-only" \
  available="`NOT install-hardened-stable-kernel`" \
  default-value=#true {
    sysctl {
        - "kernel.kexec_load_disabled" "1"
    }
  }

- name="sysctl-bpf-disabled" \
  dialog-name="System Hardening" \
  short-description="Disable unprivileged BPF (kernel.unprivileged_bpf_disabled=1)" \
  long-description="Disable Unprivileged BPF: Restricts the BPF JIT compiler to root. BPF is a powerful in-kernel virtual machine that can be abused for attacks such as heap spraying. Normal users and applications rarely need direct BPF access. This does not affect ditana-userns-guard, which is loaded by a system service running as root." \
  spdx-identifiers="GPL-2.0-only" \
  default-value=#true {
    sysctl {
        - "kernel.unprivileged_bpf_disabled" "1"
    }
  }

- name="sysctl-ptrace-restrict" \
  dialog-name="System Hardening" \
  short-description="Restrict ptrace to root (kernel.yama.ptrace_scope=2)" \
  long-description="Restrict ptrace to Root: Limits the ptrace system call to processes running as root. ptrace allows inspecting and modifying the memory of running processes, which can be used to extract passwords or inject code. This setting will prevent non-root users from using debuggers such as gdb and strace. Developers who need debugging tools should disable this option. The Linux default (0) allows unrestricted ptrace between processes of the same user." \
  spdx-identifiers="GPL-2.0-only" \
  available="`NOT sysctl-ptrace-unrestricted`" \
  default-value="`NOT profile-developer`" {
    sysctl {
        - "kernel.yama.ptrace_scope" "2"
    }
  }

- name="sysctl-kptr-restrict" \
  dialog-name="System Hardening" \
  short-description="Hide kernel pointers (kernel.kptr_restrict=2)" \
  long-description="Hide Kernel Pointers: Prevents all users, including root, from reading kernel symbol addresses via /proc/kallsyms. These addresses can help attackers develop exploits targeting specific kernel functions. This setting has no impact on normal system operation." \
  spdx-identifiers="GPL-2.0-only" \
  default-value=#true {
    sysctl {
        - "kernel.kptr_restrict" "2"
    }
  }

- name="sysctl-dmesg-restrict" \
  dialog-name="System Hardening" \
  short-description="Restrict kernel log access to root (kernel.dmesg_restrict=1)" \
  long-description="Restrict Kernel Log Access: Prevents unprivileged users from reading kernel log messages via dmesg. The kernel log can reveal hardware details, loaded modules, and error messages that help attackers map out the system. Administrators can still access logs via journalctl or by running dmesg as root." \
  spdx-identifiers="GPL-2.0-only" \
  default-value=#true {
    sysctl {
        - "kernel.dmesg_restrict" "1"
    }
  }

- name="disable-core-dumps" \
  dialog-name="System Hardening" \
  short-description="Disable Core Dumps" \
  long-description="""
Disable Core Dumps:
Core dumps capture the memory state of a program during crashes, which is useful for debugging but may inadvertently expose sensitive information. In Ditana, core dumps are disabled by default, safeguarding system integrity and user privacy. Advanced users can choose to enable core dumps temporarily in shell sessions or for specific systemd services, ensuring a secure default configuration while allowing flexibility for debugging needs.
""" \
  spdx-identifiers="AGPL-3.0-or-later" \
  default-value="`NOT profile-developer`" {
    files "/etc/security/limits.d/50-ditana.conf" \
          "/etc/systemd/system.conf.d/50-ditana-limits.conf"
  }

- name="sysctl-fs-protection" \
  dialog-name="System Hardening" \
  short-description="Protect FIFOs and regular files in sticky directories (fs.protected_fifos/regular=2)" \
  long-description="Protect FIFOs and Regular Files in Sticky Directories: Prevents users from writing to FIFOs or regular files they do not own in world-writable sticky directories such as /tmp. This mitigates a class of privilege escalation attacks where an attacker creates or replaces files in shared directories to trick privileged processes into reading or writing attacker-controlled data. The Linux default protects hardlinks and symlinks (fs.protected_hardlinks=1, fs.protected_symlinks=1) but leaves FIFOs and regular files unprotected." \
  spdx-identifiers="GPL-2.0-only" \
  default-value=#true {
    sysctl {
        - "fs.protected_fifos" "2"
        - "fs.protected_regular" "2"
    }
  }

- name="sysctl-net-hardening" \
  dialog-name="System Hardening" \
  short-description="Harden network stack (syncookies, no redirects, no source routing, rp_filter)" \
  long-description="""
Harden Network Stack: Applies several network protections in a single setting:

• SYN flood protection via TCP syncookies.
• Reject ICMP redirects to prevent man-in-the-middle attacks (IPv4 and IPv6).
• Disable source routing, which allows senders to dictate the packet path through the network.
• Enable reverse-path filtering to drop packets with spoofed source addresses.

These settings are broadly recommended and unlikely to cause compatibility issues.
""" \
  spdx-identifiers="GPL-2.0-only" \
  default-value=#true {
    sysctl {
        - "net.ipv4.tcp_syncookies" "1"
        - "net.ipv4.conf.all.accept_redirects" "0"
        - "net.ipv6.conf.all.accept_redirects" "0"
        - "net.ipv4.conf.all.accept_source_route" "0"
        - "net.ipv4.conf.all.rp_filter" "1"
    }
  }

- name="kernel-option-perf1" \
    dialog-name="System Hardening" \
    short-description="Allow normal users to access performance profiling tools (perf_event_paranoid=1)" \
    long-description="Allow normal users to access performance profiling tools (trade-off for developer usability)" \
    spdx-identifiers="GPL-2.0-only" \
    default-value="`profile-developer`" {
    \ // https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/sysctl/kernel.html#perf-event-paranoid
    sysctl {
        - "kernel.perf_event_paranoid" "1"
    }
  }

- name="sysctl-ptrace-unrestricted" \
  dialog-name="System Hardening" \
  short-description="Unrestrict ptrace for developers (kernel.yama.ptrace_scope=0)" \
  long-description="""
Allow unrestricted ptrace: Sets kernel.yama.ptrace_scope=0 and completely disables Yama restrictions.

This restores the classic Linux behavior where any process of the same user can debug or trace other processes of that user. **This setting is particularly important for developers** – it enables full functionality of debugging and profiling tools such as gdb, lldb, strace, perf, rr, Valgrind, SystemTap and IDE debuggers. Without this option most of these tools will fail with «Operation not permitted» or «Permission denied» when trying to attach to another process.
""" \
  spdx-identifiers="GPL-2.0-only" \
  available="`NOT sysctl-ptrace-restrict`" \
  default-value="`profile-developer`" {
    sysctl {
        - "kernel.yama.ptrace_scope" "0"
    }
  }
